"Surah al-Hujurat begins with the address, 'O you who have believed!' to draw the attention of the believers. In the first verse, it is commanded not to put oneself forward before Allah and His Messenger. The word 'taqdim' means to put something forward. This prohibition forbids acting hastily in word and deed without observing the Sunnah of the Prophet and the ruling of Allah. The verse encompasses every kind of disrespect and behavior contrary to the Sharia.\n\nThere are various narrations regarding the occasion of revelation of this verse: In one narration, it is stated that it was revealed upon the dispute that arose between Abu Bakr and Umar when the delegation of Banu Tamim arrived. According to another narration, some people had slaughtered their sacrificial animals before the Prophet on the Day of Eid al-Adha. Furthermore, it is also narrated that it was revealed when some said words such as 'if only it were like this'.\n\nIn the second verse, the believers are commanded not to raise their voices above the voice of the Prophet and not to speak loudly to him. When this verse was revealed, Abu Bakr and Umar lowered their voices before the Prophet. Thabit ibn Qays, on the other hand, worried because he thought his voice was naturally loud; however, the Prophet gave him the glad tidings that he was among the people of Paradise.\n\nIn the third verse, it is reported that those who lower their voices in the presence of the Prophet possess piety and will receive a great reward. In the fourth and fifth verses, those who call out from behind the private chambers are reproached. It is narrated that these verses were revealed concerning a delegation from the tribe of Banu Tamim. They called out, disturbing the Prophet, and this conduct of theirs was criticized.\n\nIn the sixth verse, it is commanded that if a corrupt person (fasiq / a sinful person) brings news, its accuracy must be investigated. The occasion of revelation of this verse concerns al-Walid ibn Uqbah being sent to the Banu al-Mustaliq as a collector of zakah and his bringing false news that they had apostatized from the religion. The verse counsels against believing news immediately and causing harm.\n\nIn the seventh verse, the believers are reminded that the Messenger of Allah is among them. The importance of following the Prophet is emphasized, and it is stated that if he were to follow them in many matters, they would surely fall into hardship. Then, it is stated that Allah has endeared faith to them and made disbelief, defiance, and disobedience hateful to them. This demonstrates that faith is grounded upon love and is a grace from Allah.\n\nBy Allah's grace and favor, those who remain steadfast on the path of faith and obedience are those who heed the Prophet's warnings and refrain from corruption and rebellion. These individuals are the distinguished servants whose hearts Allah has tested for piety. They are the ones rightly guided. Allah knows all things and bestows favor through His wisdom.\n\nIf two groups among the believers fight, reconciliation must be made between them immediately. This command was revealed concerning an incident that took place between Abdullah ibn Ubayy and Abdullah ibn Rawahah. If one of the parties attacks unjustly, that transgressing group must be fought until it returns to the command of Allah. When the fighting ceases, judgment must be made with justice. Although this ruling is primarily directed at those in authority, it is a collective obligation (fard al-kifayah) that concerns all believers.\n\nAll believers are brothers. For this reason, making peace between two brothers is a requirement of brotherhood in religion. Fearing Allah and preserving peace is part of piety. If there is no peace among believers, their strength to struggle against the disbelievers weakens, and they may be deprived of Allah's mercy.\n\nO you who have believed! Let not a people ridicule another people; perhaps the ridiculed are better than those who ridicule. Nor let women ridicule other women. Do not insult yourselves, and do not call each other by offensive nicknames. These prohibitions are meant to reinforce respect and brotherhood among believers. Ridicule means insult and contempt; this is contrary to the spirit of Islamic brotherhood.\n\nAvoid much suspicion, for indeed, some suspicion is a sin. Do not spy—that is, do not seek out faults—and do not backbite one another. Backbiting is saying something behind a person's back that they would dislike; this is an abhorrent act, like eating the flesh of one's dead brother. Fear Allah, for He is the Accepter of Repentance, Most Merciful.\n\nO mankind! We created you from a male and a female, and made you into nations and tribes that you may know one another. Indeed, the most noble of you in the sight of Allah is the most righteous of you. Indeed, Allah is Knowing, All-Aware.\n\nThe Bedouins said, 'We have believed,' but faith had not yet settled in their hearts. True believers are those who believe in Allah and His Messenger, then never doubt, and strive with their wealth and their lives. The reward of those who obey Allah and His Messenger is not diminished. Allah is Forgiving, Most Merciful. They should not hold their becoming Muslim as a favor; rather, they should realize that they attained faith through Allah's grace.\n\nAllah commands: O mankind! We created you from a male and a female. That is, you are all of the same origin; therefore, there is no meaning in boasting of your lineage or looking down upon others. We made you into nations and tribes that you may know one another—that is, so you may know your lineage and cooperate accordingly, not to quarrel and boast. The most noble of you in the sight of Allah is the most advanced in piety. That is, the one among you whose consciousness of responsibility toward Allah and sense of vigilance is the strongest is the most worthy.\n\nPiety (taqwa) literally means protection. It is a person's shielding themselves from sin and the punishment of Allah. Taqwa has degrees: its lowest degree is avoiding polytheism (shirk), while its highest is turning toward Allah with one's entire being and refraining from everything other than Him. To be a person of piety, avoiding the unlawful (haram) is not enough; one must also keep away from doubtful matters. For circling around the unlawful may cause a person to fall into it. Our Prophet (peace be upon him) said regarding this: What is lawful is clear, and what is unlawful is clear; and between the two are doubtful matters. Whoever avoids doubtful matters safeguards his religion and his honor.\n\nRegarding the occasion of revelation of the verse, the following narration is reported: Salman al-Farsi used to serve two Companions. One day, because he overslept, they sent him to ask for some condiment from the Prophet. When they learned that the Prophet had nothing with him, they said between themselves about Salman something like, 'If we sent him to a well, its water would run dry.' Later, when they came into the presence of the Prophet, the Messenger of Allah remarked that he saw the greenness of meat in their mouths, whereupon they stated that they had not eaten meat. Upon this, the Prophet said, 'You must have backbitten,' and this verse was revealed.\n\nThen the verse draws attention to the difference between faith and Islam. The Bedouins said, 'We have believed.' Say to them: You have not believed; rather say, 'We have submitted in Islam.' For faith is the affirmation of the heart; it has not yet entered your hearts. True believers are those who believe in Allah and His Messenger, then do not doubt, and strive with their wealth and their lives in the cause of Allah. They are the truthful ones. Allah's knowledge encompasses all things; whatever is hidden and whatever is open, He knows. Therefore, He will judge you according to your intentions.\n\nFinally, addressing those who held their becoming Muslim as a favor over the Prophet, it is declared: Do not consider your Islam a favor to me. Rather, Allah has conferred a favor upon you, for He has guided you to faith. If you are truthful, this is a great blessing. Allah knows the unseen of the heavens and the earth; He sees everything you do. Be upright, then, from inside out, and protect yourselves through piety."